The Rust binding links the installed static pancake archives directly and
mirrors the à-la-carte archive set with Cargo features: the crate root always
links `libpancake_ocapn` and presents a typed API in
[`bindings/rust/src/lib.rs`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/rust/src/lib.rs),
and each transport is its own feature-gated module — `tcp` and `inproc` on by
default, `iroh` opt-in — so a build links exactly the archives its features turn
on (the TCP `Netlayer`/`Listener`/`Endpoint` live under `pancake::tcp`). You work
with a `Peer` and Rust enums for `Reference`, `Argument`, and `Event`, so the
wire's value tree arrives as ordinary Rust values you can match on.

## The same round-trip

The round-trip the [Start building]($link.page('start')) page walks through in Zig —
connect, fetch a capability by name, call it, and handle the reply — reads like this in
Rust. The `Connection` owns the peer on its own task, so it runs inside a
`tokio::task::LocalSet`.

```rust
use pancake::{connect, Argument};

let conn = connect("ocapn://127.0.0.1:9099.tcp").await?; // the partner's locator
let greeter = conn.fetch("greeter").await?; // a capability, by name
match conn.send(&greeter, "greet", &[Argument::String("world".into())]).await {
    Ok(Argument::String(reply)) => println!("{reply}"),
    Ok(other) => println!("unexpected reply: {other:?}"),
    Err(reason) => eprintln!("the greeter refused: {reason:?}"),
}
conn.close();
```

## Async

The async surface is [tokio](https://tokio.rs/), behind an opt-in `async` feature. An `AsyncConnection`
runs the sans-io peer over a `tokio::net::TcpStream`. Because the peer is a
single-owner state machine, the `Connection` is `!Send`: you create and use it on
one task inside a `LocalSet`, which keeps the peer calls synchronous and lets only the
socket reads and writes await. A `tell` returns once the deliver is queued on that
driver task, and the task flushes it.

## Actors

The actor layer is an `actors::Vat` over `FnMut(&str, &[Argument])` closures. A
local actor is a Rust closure, not a foreign callback, and a leading symbol
argument is the method verb. An eventual-send to a partner-hosted reference travels
the wire and settles through `Vat::send(...).await`.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build rust-dogfood    # cargo test against the pancake archives, sync + async
```

The full surface lives in the
[Rust binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/rust/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/rust/client.rs)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
